<ArticleLayout slug="what-a-finished-brand-asset-costs" ctaLabel="Get a market range for your volume">
  <p class="standfirst">Every page that answers this question gives you a range without telling you what one asset is, where the number came from, or who paid for the research. This one defines the unit first, publishes absolute figures, and shows the method. No email gate.</p>

  <h2>What does one finished brand asset cost?</h2>

  <p>Between roughly $0.25 for a bulk ecommerce image edit and several thousand for a directed brand film, and the spread is that wide because "one asset" means six different things depending on who is quoting. Published studio rate cards collected in September 2026 put a standard product image at $39 to $50 and a short video clip at about $93. hubStudio published this page because the category will not.</p>

  <p>Read the definition below before the numbers. Skipping it is how buyers end up comparing a quote for a photograph against a quote for a campaign.</p>

  <h2>What counts as one finished asset?</h2>

  <p>Nobody has settled this. There is no ISO standard, no trade-body definition, and the three published conventions that come closest draw the line in three different places for three different purposes.</p>

  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th scope="col">Convention</th>
          <th scope="col">What it treats as one unit</th>
          <th scope="col">What it does not count as new</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">The asset identity registry</th>
          <td>One registered advertising asset, coded and carried across platforms</td>
          <td>No published rule on lengths, cuts, language versions or resizes</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">The production bid form</th>
          <td>One production, estimated across sections A to P, with stills and social bid as separate breakouts</td>
          <td>Alternate setups shot inside the same production</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <th scope="row">The performers' union agreement</th>
          <td>One commercial, for session-fee purposes</td>
          <td>Alternate scenes shot in the same session, and edits inside defined parameters</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <p>Three conventions, three boundaries. A resize is a new asset under none of them explicitly, and a new asset under all of them in practice, because somebody has to make it.</p>

  <p><strong>So here is the definition this page uses, stated openly because the industry has not supplied one.</strong> One finished asset is one deliverable file, in one specified format and one specified duration or dimension, approved for use and cleared for the channel it will run on.</p>

  <p>Under that definition a resize is a new asset. A language version is a new asset. An alternate cut is a new asset. An alternate take shot in the same session is not.</p>

  <p>You may disagree with that line. That is fine, and it is the point: you now know where it sits, which is more than any quote you receive will tell you. Every band below is quoted against the unit its own source card states, and where that differs from the definition above, the page says so.</p>

  <h2>What do published rate cards actually say?</h2>

  <p>These are read directly from published pricing pages, one card at a time, in September 2026. Not aggregated, not estimated, not taken from a roundup.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The floor for ecommerce image editing sits between roughly $0.25 and $1.20 an image on published cards in this category, with one card adding a fixed platform fee of about $95 a month on top.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: published pricing pages in this category, collected September 2026, two independent cards read directly and compared, no vendor named.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Published studio rate cards put a standard product image at $39 to $50, a model or lifestyle image at about $95, a short video clip at about $93 and a single UGC video at about $89, with editing add-ons around $49 each and a finished pro edit at about $199.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: published pricing pages in this category, collected September 2026, two independent cards read directly, prices quoted as each card states them.</p>

  <p>Put those two blockquotes side by side and the first useful thing appears. The gap between an image edit at $0.25 and a product image at $39 is not a negotiating range. It is two different products wearing the same noun.</p>

