  <p>Retouch is not production. It is the cheapest line on this page by two orders of magnitude, and it is the line most often quoted when a buyer asks what content costs.</p>

  <h2>What is inside a per-asset price?</h2>

  <p>The strongest evidence on this comes from the production industry's own paperwork, and it contains an absence worth more than most of the figures.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The US commercial production trade body's standard bid form itemizes a production across sections A through P, from prep crew to breakout talent, and carries no line at all for media usage payments.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the trade body's own free bid form, updated January 2023, downloaded and read in full September 2026.</p>

  <p>Sixteen sections covering everything from prep to talent, and usage is not one of them. Usage is what you pay to keep running the asset, and the standard document for pricing production does not price it. So a production quote and the cost of having that asset in market are different numbers, and the industry's own form is built on that assumption.</p>

  <p>The same body is explicit about the question buyers should be asking.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">The same trade body's 2026 bidding guide tells bidders to establish whether a stated budget is all in, and whether stills, social elements and behind the scenes carry separate decks and separate budgets.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the trade body's published bidding reference guide, 2026 edition.</p>

  <p>Stills, social and behind the scenes as separate budgets. If you have ever received a film quote and then a second invoice for the social cutdowns, that is not a vendor being sharp. That is the category's standard structure.</p>

  <p>Talent adds a third layer with its own boundary rules.</p>

  <blockquote class="evidence">Under the US performers' union commercials agreement effective 1 April 2025, alternate scenes shot for one commercial in the same session are not treated as new commercials, while an edit or lift outside the defined parameters creates a new commercial and new fees.</blockquote>
  <p class="source">Source: the union commercials agreement effective April 2025, as read by two independent law firms. The union's own rate documents are not machine readable.</p>

  <p>An edit outside the parameters creates a new commercial. Not a new file. A new commercial, with new fees attached. That is a versioning cost that lives entirely outside the production quote.</p>

  <p>So the honest anatomy of a per-asset cost has four layers, and most published figures cover only the first:</p>

